// English text for the articles in knowledge.js: id → [title, body]. Add one line here when adding an article there.
// i18n.js maps the Vietnamese title and body to these strings when the site is in English.
const KNOWLEDGE_EN = {
  'op-principles': ["Opening principles", "The first ten moves shape the game. (1) Occupy or control the centre (e4, d4, e5, d5) with pawns and pieces. (2) Develop minor pieces first: usually knights, then bishops, each to a good square the first time. (3) Castle early, usually before move 10. (4) Do not move a piece twice without a reason (capturing, avoiding being trapped). (5) Do not bring the queen out too early. (6) Connect the rooks, then put them on open or half-open files. (7) Do not grab wing pawns while your pieces are undeveloped. Each move should do at least one of these things."],
  'op-early-queen': ["Early queen moves: why to avoid them", "The queen is your most valuable piece, so when it comes out early the opponent develops minor pieces and chases it, and each chase is a free developing move (a lost tempo). For example, after 1.e4 e5 2.Qh5 Nc6 3.Bc4 Black must stop mate on f7 with 3...g6 (or 3...Qe7), not 3...Nf6?? because of 4.Qxf7#. After 3...g6 4.Qf3 Nf6 the white queen has to move again. Only bring the queen out early for a concrete reason: a capture that goes unpunished, or a clear tactic."],
  'op-castle-plan': ["When and which way to castle", "Castling puts the king in a safe corner and brings a rook toward the centre. Kingside castling (O-O) is usually faster: you only need to move the g-knight and f-bishop. Queenside castling (O-O-O) puts the rook straight on the d-file, but the king on c1 is a little exposed and needs the a2-pawn covered or a king move to b1. When the two sides castle on opposite wings, the game becomes a pawn-storm race toward the enemy king. Do not leave your king in the middle once the centre has opened: that is when you are easiest to mate."],
  'op-f7': ["The weak f7 (f2) square in the opening", "Early in the game, Black’s f7 square (White’s f2) is defended only by the king, so it is a target for a bishop on c4, a knight on g5 and the queen. The fastest traps (Scholar’s Mate, the Fried Liver: 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.Ng5 d5 5.exd5 Nxd5? 6.Nxf7) all aim at f7. To defend: play ...Nf6 in time, castle early, and as Black in the Two Knights, answer 5.exd5 with 5...Na5 instead of recapturing with the knight."],
  'op-traps': ["Opening traps beginners often meet", "Scholar’s Mate: 1.e4 e5 2.Bc4 Nc6 3.Qh5 Nf6?? 4.Qxf7#; defend with 3...g6 or 3...Qe7. Fool’s Mate: 1.f3 e5 2.g4?? Qh4#; do not open the diagonal to your king. Légal’s Trap: 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 d6 3.Bc4 Bg4 4.Nc3 g6? 5.Nxe5! Bxd1?? 6.Bxf7+ Ke7 7.Nd5#. The general lesson: a pin does not always hold a piece, and whenever an opponent “gifts” you material, ask why."],
  'op-gambit': ["Gambits: giving a pawn for speed", "A gambit gives up a pawn (sometimes more) in the opening in exchange for fast development, open files or an attack on the king. The side making the sacrifice must keep playing actively; if the opponent gets to develop and castle, all that remains is a missing pawn. The side accepting should return the pawn at the right moment for development and king safety instead of clinging to it at all costs. Examples: the Queen’s Gambit (if Black takes on c4, White usually regains the pawn easily), the King’s Gambit, the Evans Gambit, the Danish Gambit."],
  'op-repertoire': ["Choosing a repertoire for your level", "Below about 900 Elo: play the Italian Game as White, answer 1.e4 with 1...e5 and 1.d4 with 1...d5 (Queen’s Gambit Declined), and learn principles rather than memorising moves. From about 900 to 1400: the London System as White (easy to set up, little theory), the Caro-Kann against 1.e4, and the Queen’s Gambit Declined against 1.d4. Above 1400: the Ruy Lopez, Sicilian and Nimzo-Indian give positions richer in plans. Pick few openings, play them many times, then review your losses in the History page."],
  'op-transition': ["After the opening: moving into the middlegame", "Once your pieces are out and your king has castled, ask three questions: on which wing does the pawn structure let me play (usually where my pawn chain points)? Which file is about to open for my rooks? Which of my pieces is worst and can be improved? Do not push pawns aimlessly just because you “don’t know what to play”. A small, concrete plan (bringing a knight to an outpost, doubling rooks on an open file) is better than no plan."],
  'tac-values': ["Piece values and exchanges", "Approximate values: pawn 1, knight 3, bishop 3 (the bishop pair is usually worth a little more), rook 5, queen 9; the king is priceless. Giving a rook for a knight or bishop is called “losing the exchange” (about 2 points). Three minor pieces are often stronger than a queen in the middlegame, and two rooks are roughly equal to a queen. These are only estimates: an active piece attacking the king can be worth far more than its number."],
  'tac-hanging': ["Hanging pieces: count attackers and defenders", "A piece is “hanging” when it is attacked and undefended, or attacked by a cheaper piece. Before each move, count: how many enemy pieces attack that square, and how many of yours defend it? If attackers outnumber defenders, or the attacker is cheaper than the piece attacked, that piece is in danger. Do not forget that the piece you just moved may have stopped defending something else. This is the most common mistake below 1200 Elo."],
  'tac-cct': ["Checks, captures, threats (CCT)", "Every turn, before thinking about plans, look in this order: all Checks, all Captures, all Threats (attacking undefended pieces, threatening mate). Do this for both sides: your strong moves and your opponent’s dangerous ones. Most tactics, and most blunders, fall into these three groups of moves. Seeing the tactic only after you have moved is too late."],
  'tac-fork': ["The fork (double attack)", "A fork is one piece attacking two (or more) targets at once, so the opponent can save only one. Knights fork best because they jump and cannot be blocked: look for a square where the knight checks the king and hits the queen or a rook. Pawns fork two minor pieces (for example d4-d5 hitting a knight on c6 and a bishop on e6). Queens fork with a check that also attacks an undefended piece. Warning signs: two enemy pieces a knight’s move apart, or undefended on the same rank or diagonal. A familiar example: a white knight on c7 checks the king on e8 and hits the rook on a8 (a king-and-rook fork); a knight on f7 attacks the queen on d8 and the rook on h8 together."],
  'tac-pin': ["The pin", "A pin is when a bishop, rook or queen attacks a piece with a more valuable piece behind it. Absolute pin: the piece behind is the king, so the pinned piece may not move. Relative pin: the piece behind is the queen or a rook, so the pinned piece may move but the piece behind is lost. To exploit a pin: attack the pinned piece with a pawn or cheap piece and pile up on it. To escape a pin: block the line, chase the pinning piece, or move the piece behind off the line. Only long-range pieces (bishop, rook, queen) can pin; knights, pawns and kings cannot. Absolute pin example: 1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Bb4, the bishop on b4 pins the knight on c3 to the king on e1 (Nimzo-Indian Defence). Relative pin example: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bg5, the bishop on g5 pins the knight on f6 to the queen on d8 (Queen’s Gambit Declined)."],
  'tac-skewer': ["The skewer", "A skewer is like a pin in reverse: the more valuable piece stands in front. When it moves away, the piece behind is captured. A typical example: a rook or bishop checks the king, and when the king moves a rook or queen standing behind it on the same rank or diagonal is lost. For example, a white rook on a6 checks the black king on d6 along the sixth rank; when the king leaves that rank, the rook takes the black queen on h6. It is common in endgames when the king and queen (or rook) stand in line."],
  'tac-discovered': ["Discovered check and double check", "Discovered check: a piece moves away to open a line for the piece behind it to check the king, while the moved piece is free to attack something else, often winning the queen. Double check: both pieces give check, so the opponent must move the king because both cannot be blocked or captured. Double check is the strongest move in tactics and often leads to mate. Watch for your piece standing between your own long-range piece (rook, bishop, queen) and the enemy king."],
  'tac-defender': ["Removing the defender", "If a piece or square is held by only one defender, capture, chase or exchange that defender first. For example, a knight on f6 covers h7: trade your bishop for the knight on f6, then the queen mates on h7. The question to ask: “What is stopping me from winning? Can that piece be removed?”"],
  'tac-overload': ["The overloaded piece", "A piece is overloaded when it has two jobs at once, for example guarding the back rank and defending another piece. Force it to do one job (usually with a capture or check) and the other is left undone. Look for an enemy piece defending several things, then attack one of them."],
  'tac-deflection': ["Deflection and decoy", "Deflection: force a defender away from an important post (usually with a sacrifice it must capture). Decoy: lure the enemy king or queen to a bad square where it can be forked, skewered or mated. Both usually begin with a sacrifice, so only play them once you have calculated to the end."],
  'tac-interference': ["Interference", "Place a piece on the line between a defender and the square or piece it protects, cutting off the defence. It often appears when two enemy rooks, or a rook and queen, protect each other along a rank. The blocking piece is usually capturable, but capturing it abandons another duty."],
  'tac-zwischenzug': ["The in-between move (zwischenzug)", "When the opponent captures and expects you to recapture, there is sometimes a stronger move to play first: a check, a bigger threat, or another capture. An in-between move upsets the “take and retake” calculation. Before recapturing by reflex, look at checks and threats first."],
  'tac-xray': ["X-ray", "A long-range piece (rook, bishop, queen) still “sees through” a piece to attack or defend the square behind it. For example, with two rooks doubled on a file, the rear rook still defends the square the front rook moves to. When counting attackers and defenders, remember to include pieces standing behind along the X-ray."],
  'tac-trapped': ["Trapping pieces", "A piece with no safe squares can be won by attacking it. Common cases: a bishop takes the a7 (a2) pawn and is shut in by a pawn on b6 (b3); a knight on the edge has no way back; a queen greedily takes the b2 (b7) pawn and is surrounded by rooks and minor pieces. Before sending a piece deep into enemy territory, check its way home."],
  'tac-promotion': ["Promotion tactics and underpromotion", "A pawn reaching the last rank promotes to a queen, rook, bishop or knight. Promotion tactics often use a sacrifice to pull away a blocker or to cut off a piece defending the promotion square. Underpromotion has its reasons: promote to a knight to give check (and fork) at once, or to a rook or bishop to avoid stalemating the opponent."],
  'tac-sacrifice': ["Sacrifices: when they are worth it", "A sacrifice is worth it when it gains something concrete: mate, more material back, promotion, or an attack that is hard to meet. Positional sacrifices “by feel” need experience; below about 1600, only sacrifice when you can calculate the result. Always check your opponent’s best defence, not just your own prettiest line."],
  'mate-backrank': ["Back-rank mate", "A castled king behind unmoved f, g and h pawns has no escape square; a rook or queen reaching the back rank is mate. Warning sign: the opponent’s back rank is held by only one piece (easy to overload or deflect). Defence: make “luft” with h3 (h6) or g3 (g6) when you have time, and keep a rook on the back rank when needed."],
  'mate-smothered': ["Smothered mate (knight)", "The king is boxed in by its own pieces, and a knight check is mate. The classic pattern (Philidor’s Legacy) with the black king on h8: Nf7+ Kg8, Nh6+ (double check) Kh8, Qg8+! Rxg8, Nf7#. Warning signs: king in the corner, rook and pawns around it unmoved, and the queen’s a2-g8 diagonal open."],
  'mate-arabian': ["Arabian mate", "A rook and knight work together in the corner: the enemy king is on h8, a knight on f6 protects a rook on h7 and covers g8, and the rook gives check along the h-file or seventh rank. The knight often arrives on f6 after a sacrifice or by taking a g7 or h7 pawn."],
  'mate-anastasia': ["Anastasia’s mate", "A knight on e7 covers g8 and g6, the opponent’s own g7 pawn blocks the king on h7, and a rook or queen gives check along the h-file. It often begins with a queen sacrifice on h7 to open the h-file."],
  'mate-boden': ["Boden’s mate (two bishops)", "Two criss-crossing bishops mate a king castled queenside when the squares beside it are blocked by its own pieces (for example a rook on d8 and a knight on d7). The typical pattern: king on c8, a bishop on f4 covering b8 and d6, and the other bishop giving check on a6. It often involves a queen sacrifice on c6 or b7 to open the diagonal."],
  'mate-greek-gift': ["The Greek Gift (bishop sacrifice on h7)", "Bxh7+! Kxh7, Ng5+, then Qh5 (or Qd3/Qc2) attacking h7. Usual requirements: a bishop aiming at h7 (bishop on d3), a knight that can safely jump to g5, a queen that can reach h5, a pawn on e5 that has driven away the f6-knight (so Black has no kingside defender), and no good ...Bf5 or ...Qxg5 for Black. Calculate the king retreats to g8, g6 and h6 carefully before sacrificing."],
  'mate-queen-pawn': ["Queen and pawn mate (Damiano style)", "A pawn on f6 or h6 protecting a queen on g7 is mate against a king on g8 (when nothing can capture the queen). This pattern appears once a pawn has advanced deep next to the enemy king. An open h-file with queen and rook also often gives Qh7# or Qh8#."],
  'mate-epaulette': ["Epaulette mate", "The king is hemmed in on both sides by its own pieces (for example rooks on d8 and f8 around a king on e8). A queen check from the front (e6), controlling d7 and f7, is mate. Warning sign: an uncastled king with heavy pieces on both sides."],
  'mate-basic': ["Basic mates: queen, rook, two rooks", "Queen + king: use the queen to shrink the “box” around the enemy king (standing a knight’s move away), push it to the edge, then bring your king closer and mate; always leave the enemy king a square to avoid stalemate. Rook + king: the rook cuts off a rank, and when the kings face each other the rook checks to drive the king back one rank; repeat. Two rooks: give staircase checks, with no help needed from the king."],
  'mate-spot': ["How to spot mate in a game", "When the enemy king has few escape squares, list all your checks, including sacrifices. For each one, count the squares the king can go to and the pieces that can block or capture the checker. Memorising patterns (back rank, smothered, Arabian, Boden, queen + pawn) lets you see them faster than calculating every move. Conversely, when your own king is hemmed in, make luft first."],
  'st-open-file': ["Rooks on open files and the seventh rank", "An open file has no pawns; a rook placed there controls the whole file and can invade. A half-open file (only enemy pawns left) lets the rook attack that pawn. Double rooks (or rook and queen) on an open file to win control of it. A rook on the seventh rank (the opponent’s second rank) eats pawns and confines the king, and is very strong in the endgame."],
  'st-outpost': ["Outposts for knights", "An outpost is a square in the enemy half that enemy pawns can never chase a piece from, ideally protected by your own pawn (for example a knight on d5 or e5). A knight on an outpost is often as strong as a rook. Create outposts by exchanging or fixing the pawns that could chase the knight; fight them by exchanging a piece for that knight."],
  'st-bishops': ["Good bishop, bad bishop and the bishop pair", "A “bad” bishop is blocked by its own pawns (pawns on squares of its colour); a “good” bishop has open diagonals. Put your pawns on the opposite colour to your remaining bishop, or exchange the bad bishop. The bishop pair is strong in open positions; knights are stronger in closed positions with many pawns and outposts."],
  'st-iqp': ["The isolated pawn (isolated d-pawn)", "An isolated pawn has no friendly pawns on the neighbouring files, so no pawn can protect it. The side with the isolated pawn (usually on d4) gets space, the e5 and c5 squares for knights, and half-open files, so should play actively, attack the king, and sometimes break with d4-d5. The side playing against it should blockade the square directly in front of the pawn (d5) with a piece, ideally a knight, exchange minor pieces, then attack the pawn in the endgame."],
  'st-pawn-weak': ["Doubled, backward and hanging pawns", "Doubled pawns (two on one file) struggle to defend themselves and cannot create a passed pawn, but often come with a half-open file. A backward pawn has been left behind, cannot advance safely, and the square in front of it is weak for the opponent. Hanging pawns (a pair of c and d pawns side by side with no pawn support) are strong while many pieces remain but easily become targets once pieces are exchanged. Attack weak pawns with heavy pieces on half-open files."],
  'st-passed': ["Passed pawns", "A passed pawn has no enemy pawn blocking or controlling its path to the promotion square. “Passed pawns must be pushed.” A passed pawn protected by a pawn is very durable; an outside passed pawn (far from the kings) drags the enemy king away so your king can take the pawns on the other wing. Blockade an enemy passed pawn with a piece directly in front of it (a knight is best)."],
  'st-majority': ["Pawn majorities and creating a passed pawn", "On the wing where you have more pawns than the opponent (for example 3 against 2 on the queenside), push the pawn with no pawn opposite it first (the candidate) to create a passed pawn. A queenside majority is especially strong when both sides have castled kingside, because the passed pawn appears far from the enemy king."],
  'st-chain': ["Pawn chains: attack the base", "A diagonal pawn chain (for example White’s d4-e5 in the French Defence) is strong at the head and weak at the base. Attack the base of the chain (for Black, ...c5 hitting d4, then ...f6 hitting e5). Usually play on the side your pawn chain points toward: the d4-e5 chain points to the kingside, so White attacks on the kingside and Black counterattacks on the queenside."],
  'st-weak-squares': ["Weak squares and holes", "A weak square can no longer be protected by a pawn (the neighbouring pawns have gone past it). Holes near the king, such as f6 or h6 after ...g6 when the dark-squared bishop is gone, are where enemy pieces settle to attack. Before pushing a pawn, ask: what square does this move leave that a pawn can no longer protect?"],
  'st-worst-piece': ["Improve your worst piece", "When there are no tactics, find the piece doing the least (a blocked bishop, an undeveloped rook, a knight on the rim) and bring it to a better square. A more active piece often matters more than being a pawn up or down. This simple rule gives you a plan in most quiet positions."],
  'st-exchanges': ["When to exchange pieces", "When ahead in material: exchange pieces (not pawns) to reach an easily won endgame. When behind: avoid exchanges, keep pawns and look for counterplay. When cramped: exchange some pieces to free yourself. Exchange the opponent’s attacking pieces and keep your active ones. Trading your bad bishop for the opponent’s good bishop is a gain."],
  'st-prophylaxis': ["Prophylaxis: what does the opponent want?", "After each opponent move, ask: “What does this threaten? If it were their turn again, what would they do?” Stopping the opponent’s plan before it becomes a threat (for example h3 to stop ...Bg4, a4 to stop ...b5) is usually much cheaper than defending later."],
  'st-center': ["The pawn centre and undermining it", "A pawn centre (pawns on e4 and d4) gives space and restricts enemy pieces. Hypermodern play lets the opponent build a centre and then attacks it with pieces and pawns (...c5, ...e5, ...f6). With a closed centre (pawns locked), the game moves to attacks on the wings; with an open centre, quick development and king safety matter more."],
  'st-attack-king': ["Attacking the king", "Opposite-side castling: storm the pawns toward the enemy king to open files for the rooks; the faster side usually wins. Same-side castling: attack with pieces (bringing queen, knight and bishop toward the king) rather than pushing the pawns in front of your own king. Enemy king still in the middle: open the centre with pawns, even at the cost of a sacrifice. You need more attackers than defenders near the king."],
  'end-king': ["The king is a fighting piece in the endgame", "Once queens are off and few pieces remain, the king no longer fears mate: bring it to the centre at once, and use it to protect your pawns and capture enemy ones. In pawn endgames, the more active king often decides the result."],
  'end-opposition': ["Opposition and key squares (king + pawn vs king)", "Opposition: two kings facing each other one square apart; the side not to move “has the opposition” and can push the other king back. With a pawn on the b to g files, the stronger side wins if its king reaches a key square. A pawn not yet past the middle (for example on e4) has key squares d6, e6, f6; a pawn on the fifth rank (e5) also has d7, e7, f7. Principle: the king goes ahead of the pawn; do not push the pawn too soon. With an a- or h-pawn, if the defending king reaches the corner it is usually a draw."],
  'end-square': ["The rule of the square", "Picture a square with sides running from a passed pawn to its promotion square. If the enemy king can step inside that square (counting whose move it is), it catches the pawn; if not, the pawn promotes. A pawn on its starting rank counts as if already on the next rank, because it can move two squares."],
  'end-lucena': ["The Lucena position: “building a bridge”", "Rook + pawn on the seventh rank vs rook, with the stronger king on the promotion square and the enemy king cut off by one file. How to win: bring your rook to the fourth rank (counted from your side), let your king step off the promotion square; when the enemy rook checks along the file, your king walks toward your rook, and then your rook blocks the check on the fourth rank. This is the most important winning position in rook endgames."],
  'end-philidor': ["The Philidor position: rook defence", "Rook + pawn vs rook, with the defending king in front of the pawn. Put your rook on the third rank from your side (the sixth rank for Black) so the enemy king cannot advance. When the pawn moves up to that rank, your rook immediately goes to the opponent’s back rank and checks from behind. Holding this setup draws."],
  'end-rook-rules': ["Rook endgame principles", "Put rooks behind passed pawns (your own to push them, the opponent’s to restrain them), because the rook gets more squares as the pawn advances. An active rook matters more than a pawn: a passive rook just guarding a pawn often loses. Use your rook to cut off the enemy king along a rank or file. Many rook endgames a pawn down are still drawn."],
  'end-wrong-bishop': ["The wrong-coloured bishop with an a- or h-pawn", "Bishop + a- (or h-) pawn cannot win if the bishop does not control the promotion square (the corner’s colour is different) and the defending king has reached the corner: the result is a draw. A defender who is behind in material should aim for this; the stronger side should keep another pawn or keep the enemy king out of the corner."],
  'end-opposite-bishops': ["Opposite-coloured bishop endgames", "Each side has only one bishop, on opposite colours: draws are very likely, even a pawn or two down, because the defender blockades with king or bishop on squares the enemy bishop cannot reach. With other pieces added (queen, rook) the reverse holds: the attacker is stronger, because the defending bishop cannot cover the other colour."],
  'end-stalemate': ["Stalemate: avoid it when winning, seek it when losing", "Stalemate is when the side to move is not in check but has no legal move: the game is drawn. The winning side (especially with a queen) must always check that the enemy king still has a square. The losing side should try to create stalemate: put the king in the corner and sacrifice the remaining pieces."],
  'end-queen-pawn7': ["Queen vs pawn on the seventh rank", "A queen beats a pawn on the seventh rank supported by its king if it is a b, d, e or g pawn: check repeatedly to force the king in front of the pawn, then bring your king closer. A c or f pawn (or a or h pawn) usually draws thanks to a stalemate trick, unless the stronger king is already close."],
  'end-zugzwang': ["Zugzwang and triangulation", "Zugzwang: the side to move must make its own position worse. It is common in pawn endgames. Triangulation: the king makes three moves in a triangle to “lose a tempo”, handing the move back to the opponent and putting them in zugzwang. Keeping a spare pawn move in reserve is another way to win the tempo battle."],
  'end-convert': ["Converting an advantage into a win", "When ahead in material: (1) safety first: do not allow mate or surprise tactics; (2) exchange pieces, keep pawns; (3) bring your king up once queens are off; (4) create a passed pawn and push it with support from your pieces; (5) on every move check for stalemate and enemy checks. There is no rush: a slow, sure plan beats a hasty tactic."],
  'end-minor': ["Knight or bishop in the endgame", "A bishop is stronger with pawns on both wings and an open position, because it travels far quickly. A knight is stronger when pawns are on only one wing, the position is closed, or it has a fixed good square. A knight is very poor against an a- or h-file passed pawn. A lone knight or bishop (with no pawns) cannot force mate."],
  'rule-castling': ["The castling rule", "The king moves two squares toward the rook, and the rook hops over to stand right beside it. You may castle only if: neither the king nor that rook has moved; there are no pieces between them; the king is not in check; and the king does not pass through or land on an attacked square. It is still allowed if the rook is attacked or passes over an attacked square (such as b1 when castling queenside)."],
  'rule-en-passant': ["En passant", "When an enemy pawn moves two squares from its starting rank and lands right next to your pawn (a white pawn on the fifth rank, a black pawn on the fourth), your pawn may capture it as if it had moved only one square. You may do this only on the very next move, or the right is lost."],
  'rule-promotion': ["The promotion rule", "A pawn reaching the last rank must promote to a queen, rook, bishop or knight of the same colour (not a king, and it cannot stay a pawn). You may have several queens. On this site, turn on “Auto-queen” in Settings so you are not asked every time."],
  'rule-draws': ["Types of draw", "A game is drawn by: stalemate (the side to move is not in check but has no moves); the same position occurring 3 times; 50 consecutive moves by each side with no capture and no pawn move; insufficient material to mate (king vs king, king + bishop or king + knight vs king); or agreement. Running out of time when the opponent lacks the material to mate is also a draw."],
  'rule-check': ["Check, escaping check and checkmate", "When in check, your next move must escape it in one of three ways: move the king to a safe square, capture the checking piece, or block the line of check (a knight’s check and a double check cannot be blocked). If there is no way out, it is checkmate and the game ends."],
  'rule-clock': ["Clocks and increments", "Each side has its own time budget; running out loses (unless the opponent lacks the material to mate). “10+5” means 10 minutes each, plus 5 seconds added after every move. Longer games (10 minutes or more) teach better than blitz, because you have time to check for hanging pieces and calculate."],
  'tr-thinking': ["A thinking process for each move", "1) What did the opponent just play, and what does it threaten? 2) Do I have any Checks, Captures or Threats? 3) If there are no tactics: what is the plan, and which piece is worst? 4) Choose 2-3 candidate moves and compare them. 5) Before you click: check that your move does not hang a piece or allow a check or capture (a blunder check). Step 5 takes only seconds but saves a great many games."],
  'tr-calc': ["Calculating variations", "Calculate forcing moves (checks, captures, big threats) first, because the opponent has few choices and the tree stays small. For each of your moves, find the opponent’s best reply, not the one you hope for. Stop calculating when the position is quiet (no hanging pieces, no checks), then evaluate. Practise with puzzles: try to calculate the whole solution before playing the first move."],
  'tr-review': ["Reviewing your games", "After every game, especially a loss: review it yourself first, note where you felt unsure, and only then turn on engine analysis. Find the 1-2 biggest mistakes and ask “why did I play that?” (missed a tactic, fear, time trouble, no plan). On this site: History › Analyse grades every move, and mistakes go into the Mistake Notebook for review."],
  'tr-plan-elo': ["What to study at each Elo level", "Below 1000: do not hang pieces, basic mates, opening principles. 1000-1400: daily tactics (forks, pins, skewers, discovered checks), mating patterns, king + pawn endgames. 1400-1800: middlegame plans (pawn structure, worst piece), Lucena and Philidor, deeper calculation, a fixed repertoire. Above 1800: specialised endgames, opening preparation, thorough game analysis. At every level: play long enough games and review your losses."],
  'tr-time': ["Time management", "Do not spend many minutes in familiar openings; save time for decisive positions (after a big exchange, when there is a tactic, when entering the endgame). When short of time, choose safe, simple moves. If you often lose on time, switch to games with an increment (for example 15+10)."],
  'tr-mindset': ["Mindset during play", "After losing 2-3 games in a row: take a short break before playing on, because playing while annoyed leads to careless moves. When ahead: do not relax, and check every enemy check. When losing: keep looking for ways to make things difficult, as many games are saved by an opponent’s error. Judge yourself by the quality of your moves and the lessons learned, not just by results."],
  'tr-elo': ["What is Elo?", "Elo measures relative strength: beating a higher-rated player earns many points, beating a lower-rated one earns few, and losing works the other way round. Elo fluctuating is normal; look at the trend over 20-30 games. On this site, Elo changes after each game against the computer (based on the computer’s Elo) and each online game, by at most about 40 points per game."],
  'tr-app': ["Using Royal Gambit to improve", "Learn: 20 units from basic to advanced, with “skip ahead” tests to pass units you already know. Puzzles: practise tactics by theme, plus a daily puzzle. Library: 100 openings and endgame positions, with move-by-move viewing and “Practise vs computer”. History: saves every game, with analysis grading each move. Mistake Notebook: your mistakes become puzzles reviewed on a 1-3-7-21 day schedule. Royal Gambit Assistant: a personal coach that comments on games with Stockfish 19, finds weaknesses and suggests a study plan."],
  'plan-italian': ["Italian Game: main ideas", "After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4, the bishop aims at f7. The classical plan: c3 then d4 to build a centre. The slow approach (Giuoco Pianissimo) with d3: White castles, reroutes the knight b1-d2-f1-g3, plays a4 to keep room for the bishop, and prepares d4 at the right moment. Black usually develops the bishop to c5 or e7, the knight to f6, castles, and waits for ...d5 or ...a6 / ...Ba7 to keep the bishop. A trap to remember: the Two Knights (3...Nf6) allows Ng5 attacking f7."],
  'plan-ruy': ["Ruy Lopez: main ideas", "3.Bb5 attacks the knight on c6, which is defending e5; the pressure lasts for many moves. The main line: 3...a6 4.Ba4 Nf6 5.O-O Be7 6.Re1 b5 7.Bb3 d6 8.c3 O-O 9.h3: White prepares d4, the b1-knight goes d2-f1-g3, and the bishop on b3 aims at f7. Black counterattacks on the queenside (...Na5, ...c5) or holds e5 firmly. The Berlin Defence (3...Nf6) leads to an early endgame that is very solid for Black."],
  'plan-sicilian': ["Sicilian Defence: main ideas", "1.e4 c5 creates an imbalance: Black trades the c-pawn for the d-pawn (after 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4), gaining a half-open c-file and an extra central pawn. White develops quickly and often attacks on the kingside (f4, g4, Bh6, or castling queenside and storming with pawns); Black counterattacks on the queenside with ...a6, ...b5, a rook on c8 and the c4 square. The game is very sharp: whoever is a tempo slower often loses. To avoid long theory, White can play the Alapin (2.c3)."],
  'plan-french': ["French Defence: main ideas", "1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5. In the Advance variation (3.e5), White has the d4-e5 pawn chain and extra kingside space; Black attacks the base of the chain with ...c5 (with the queen on b6 and knight on c6 hitting d4), then sometimes ...f6 hitting e5. Black’s classic weakness: the c8-bishop is blocked by the e6-pawn; try to exchange it (...b6 then ...Ba6), since it cannot be developed before e6 has been played. White usually attacks on the kingside and Black counterattacks on the queenside."],
  'plan-caro': ["Caro-Kann Defence: main ideas", "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5: Black prepares ...d5 without blocking the c8-bishop as in the French Defence. In the main line (3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5), the bishop comes out to f5 first, then ...e6, ...Nd7 and ...Nf6 follow; the pawn structure is solid with few weaknesses. In the Advance variation 3.e5 Bf5, White gains space and Black counterattacks with ...c5. It suits players who like safe positions and good endgames."],
  'plan-qgd': ["Queen’s Gambit Declined: main ideas", "1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6: Black holds d5 firmly, and the c8-bishop is the hardest piece to develop. Black usually frees the game with ...c5 or ...e5, or with ...dxc4 then ...c5 at the right moment. When White exchanges cxd5 exd5, the Carlsbad structure arises: White often plays the minority attack (b4-b5 to create a weak c6-pawn), and Black looks for kingside counterplay with pieces and the e4 square for a knight."],
  'plan-kid': ["King’s Indian Defence: main ideas", "Black puts a bishop on g7, castles, lets White build a big centre (c4, d4, e4), then strikes at it with ...e5 (or ...c5). When White pushes d5 and closes the centre, both sides race on the wings: Black attacks on the kingside with ...Ne8 / ...Nd7, ...f5, ...f4, ...g5; White attacks on the queenside with b4, c5 and by opening the c-file. The position is sharp, and the faster side usually wins."],
  'plan-london': ["London System: main ideas", "White builds nearly the same setup whatever Black plays: d4, Bf4 (before e3), e3, c3, Nf3, Nbd2, Bd3, and kingside castling. Plans: a knight on e5 combined with f4 to attack the kingside, or pushing e4 once enough pieces support it. Watch out for Black’s ...Qb6 hitting b2 (usually answered by Qb3 or Qc1). It has little theory and suits beginners, but do not play it mechanically: look at your opponent’s plan."],
  'plan-english': ["English Opening (1.c4): main ideas", "1.c4 controls d5 from the wing, is flexible, and transposes easily into 1.d4 positions. White usually fianchettoes the bishop (g3, Bg2), plays Nc3, and fights for the d5 square and the queenside (b4-b5). Against 1...e5 it is like a “reversed Sicilian” with White a tempo up. It suits players who like strategic positions with less direct fighting early on."],
  'plan-scandi': ["Scandinavian Defence: main ideas", "1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3: the queen comes out early but has a safe post (Qa5 or Qd6); in return, Black challenges the centre at once and has a simple development scheme (Nf6, Bf5 or Bg4, c6, e6). White gains development by chasing the queen, so Black must play accurately in the first 10 moves."],
  'tac-clearance': ["Clearance sacrifice", "One of your own pieces is blocking a line or occupying a square another piece needs. Sacrifice or move that piece (even with a check or capture) to clear the way for your queen, rook, bishop or knight to reach a decisive square. Warning sign: “if only this piece weren’t here, I’d have a tactic.”"],
  'tac-desperado': ["The desperado", "When a piece is certain to be lost (trapped, or in the middle of an exchange), let it capture as much as possible before it goes, often with a check so the opponent has no time. It is common when both sides have pieces under attack: do not rush to recapture; check what your doomed piece can “desperately” take."],
  'tac-windmill': ["The windmill", "A combination of repeated discovered checks and ordinary checks: a piece (usually a rook) keeps giving discovered check and returning, capturing an enemy piece each time, while the enemy king only shuffles between two squares. Torre - Lasker (Moscow 1925) is the most famous example. Warning sign: the enemy king is stuck and your piece stands on the checking line of another bishop or rook."],
  'tac-attraction-mate': ["Luring the king to mate", "Sacrifice a piece to drag the enemy king out of its shelter (usually taking a pawn on h7, g7 or f7 with a bishop or rook), then bring pieces in with a series of checks. Only do this when you have enough attackers nearby and have checked the squares the king can run to. A forced mating sequence is worth sacrificing any piece."],
  'tac-defence': ["Defending under attack", "Count calmly: is the opponent’s threat real? Priorities in order: a check or bigger threat (counterattack), capturing the attacker, blocking the line, moving the attacked piece to a safe square, adding a defender. Exchange the opponent’s strongest attacking piece. When the position is lost, look for a “fortress” or perpetual check to draw."],
  'mate-opera': ["The “Opera Game” mate (Morphy 1858)", "A rook mates on the back rank, protected from afar by a bishop, when the squares around the king are blocked by its own pieces. The game Morphy - Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard (Paris 1858) ended with 16.Qb8+! Nxb8 17.Rd8#. The game’s lesson: develop quickly, open files for the rooks, and do not fear sacrifices to keep the attack going while the enemy king is still in the middle."],
  'mate-bishop-knight': ["Mate with bishop and knight", "King + bishop + knight beats a lone king, but it is difficult: you must drive the enemy king to a corner of the bishop’s colour (the other corners cannot be mated in), using the knight in a W-shaped path to push the king along the edge. It can take about 33 moves and is very rare in real games. By contrast, two knights cannot force mate against a lone king."],
  'mate-two-bishops': ["Mate with two bishops", "Two bishops side by side form a diagonal “wall” and, with your king, drive the enemy king into a corner for mate. Watch out for stalemate when the enemy king has few squares left. It usually takes about 20 moves."],
  'st-minority': ["The minority attack", "In the Carlsbad structure (White: a2, b2, d4; Black: a7, b7, c6, d5), White uses two queenside pawns to attack Black’s three with b4-b5. After the exchanges, Black usually has a weak pawn on c6 or d5 for White to attack with rooks and a knight. Black counterattacks on the kingside or uses the e4 square for a knight."],
  'st-space': ["Space advantage", "The side with more space (pawns further advanced) can easily shift pieces from one wing to the other, while the cramped side’s pieces get in each other’s way. The cramped side should exchange pieces to gain room and look for freeing pawn breaks (for example ...c5, ...f6). The side with more space should avoid exchanges and prepare a breakthrough where it is strong."],
  'st-open-closed': ["Open and closed positions", "Open positions (few central pawns, many open files): speed of development, king safety and the bishop pair matter a lot, and tactics appear often. Closed positions (locked pawn chains): knights are better than bishops, the game is slower, and the plan is to push pawns on the wings and reroute pieces to good squares. Before choosing a plan, decide whether the centre is open or closed."],
  'st-initiative': ["The initiative and tempo", "The side with the initiative keeps making threats, forcing the opponent to respond. Every move “with a threat” (a check, attacking a piece, threatening mate) gains a tempo. When you have the initiative, do not play slow moves that let the opponent breathe; when under pressure, look for moves that defend and threaten at once to win the initiative back."],
  'st-exchange-sac': ["The exchange sacrifice", "Giving a rook for a bishop or knight (about 2 points down) to get something of lasting value: wrecking the pawn structure around the enemy king, removing the best defender, gaining a very strong knight or bishop that cannot be chased, or a passed pawn. Petrosian was famous for prophylactic exchange sacrifices. Only sacrifice when you can judge the concrete compensation."],
  'st-fortress': ["The fortress (saving a lost position)", "The side behind in material builds a defensive setup the opponent cannot break despite the extra material, for example a king in the corner with a wrong-coloured bishop, or a rook + pawn holding off a queen. When losing, look for a fortress before you lose more pawns: exchange pawns that could become passed pawns for the opponent, and keep the king on key squares."],
  'st-pawn-breaks': ["Pawn breaks", "When the position is blocked, a pawn push that contacts an enemy pawn (for example ...c5, ...f5, e5, f4-f5) opens files and makes room for pieces. Prepare enough supporting pieces before pushing, because once it breaks, the newly opened files and diagonals belong to the side whose pieces are readier."],
  'end-rook-basics': ["Rook + pawn vs rook endgames: the rules", "Defending king in front of the pawn: usually a draw (the Philidor position). Defending king cut off from the pawn by two files or more: the stronger side usually wins (the Lucena position). When defending, put the king on the “short side” (the side with fewer files from the pawn) and the rook on the “long side” to check from afar. An a- or h-pawn with the rook in front of it is very hard to win."],
  'end-r-vs-minor': ["Rook vs bishop or knight (no pawns)", "Usually a draw with correct defence. Rook vs bishop: the defending king runs to the corner of the opposite colour to its bishop (the corner the bishop does not control) to avoid being driven back. Rook vs knight: keep the knight next to the king, and avoid letting the knight be separated or the king be driven into a corner."],
  'end-q-vs-r': ["Queen vs rook", "The queen beats the rook in theory but the technique is hard: drive the enemy king to the edge, use checks and waiting moves to separate the rook from the king, then win the rook with a fork or skewer. It can take many moves, and the defender always looks for stalemate and perpetual check."],
  'end-connected': ["Connected passed pawns", "Two passed pawns side by side protect each other as they advance. Two connected passed pawns on the sixth rank usually beat a rook if the enemy king is far away. Advance them level with each other, and when attacked, push the protected pawn forward first."],
  'end-breakthrough': ["The pawn breakthrough (3 vs 3)", "Three white pawns on a5, b5, c5 facing three black pawns on a7, b7, c7 (with no other pieces nearby): White to move wins with 1.b6! If 1...axb6 then 2.c6!; if 1...cxb6 then 2.a6!; one pawn always promotes. The condition: the enemy king is too far away to catch up."],
  'end-outside': ["The outside passed pawn and shouldering", "An outside passed pawn drags the enemy king to one wing, letting your king take the pawns on the other: that is why pawn endgames with an outside passed pawn usually win. “Shouldering”: use your king to block the enemy king’s path to an important area, even if you have to take a roundabout route."],
  'gen-history': ["The history of chess", "Chess originated from the game Chaturanga in India around the 6th century, spread to Persia (Shatranj), then to Europe through the Arab world. In the late 15th century the queen and bishop gained their powerful modern moves, making the game faster and sharper. The first official world championship took place in 1886. From the 1990s, computers (Deep Blue beat Kasparov in 1997) and then engines such as Stockfish changed how people train."],
  'gen-champions': ["The world champions", "In order: Steinitz, Lasker, Capablanca, Alekhine, Euwe, Botvinnik, Smyslov, Tal, Petrosian, Spassky, Fischer, Karpov, Kasparov, Kramnik, Anand, Carlsen (2013–2023), Ding Liren (2023–2024) and Gukesh Dommaraju (champion from late 2024). Each left a style worth studying: Capablanca for endgames, Tal for attacks, Petrosian for defence, Karpov for strategy, Carlsen for endgame technique. (List as of 2025.)"],
  'gen-titles': ["FIDE titles and Elo milestones", "Grandmaster (GM): 2500 Elo and 3 GM norms. International Master (IM): 2400 and 3 IM norms. FIDE Master (FM): 2300. Candidate Master (CM): 2200. Women’s titles: WGM 2300, WIM 2200, WFM 2100, WCM 2000. Elo on online sites is usually not the same as FIDE Elo; what matters is your own trend of progress."],
  'gen-notation': ["How to read chess notation", "Files a to h (left to right from White’s side), ranks 1 to 8. Piece letters (English): K king, Q queen, R rook, B bishop, N knight; pawns have no letter. Nf3: knight to f3; exd5: e-pawn captures on d5; O-O / O-O-O: castling kingside / queenside; e8=Q: promotes to a queen; + check, # checkmate. Evaluation symbols: !! brilliant, ! good, !? interesting, ?! dubious, ? mistake, ?? blunder. An evaluation of +1.0 means White is about a pawn ahead."],
  'gen-fen-pgn': ["What are FEN and PGN?", "FEN describes a position on one line: the pieces rank by rank from rank 8 down to rank 1 (numbers are empty squares, capitals are White), the side to move (w / b), castling rights (KQkq), the en passant square, the number of moves since the last capture or pawn move, and the move number. PGN stores a whole game: tournament details, player names and every move. On this site: History › download PGN, and “Set up on board” with a FEN to analyse it."],
  'gen-time': ["Time controls", "According to FIDE: blitz is at most 10 minutes per side, rapid is more than 10 and less than 60 minutes, and standard is 60 minutes or more. Online, bullet is under 3 minutes. To improve, play many rapid or standard games (10 minutes or more, with an increment) and review them, rather than only playing blitz."],
  'gen-etiquette': ["The touch-move rule and tournament etiquette", "Over the board: if you touch a piece you must move it (if it can move), and if you touch an enemy piece you must capture it; to adjust a piece, say “adjust” before touching it. Shake hands before and after the game, do not talk or disturb, and resign by laying your king down or saying you resign. Online: do not use engine help during play, and do not abandon a game when losing."],
  'gen-tournament': ["Tournament formats", "Swiss system: each round pairs players on the same score and nobody is eliminated; it is common for large events. Round robin: everyone plays everyone. Knockout: losing eliminates you (usually over 2 games, with rapid games if drawn). Tie-breaks on equal scores: Buchholz, head-to-head, number of wins."],
  'gen-vietnam': ["Chess in Vietnam", "Dao Thien Hai was Vietnam’s first grandmaster (1995). Nguyen Ngoc Truong Son earned the grandmaster title at just 14. Le Quang Liem and Truong Son have both been among the world’s top players; Liem won the World Blitz Championship in 2013. Chess is growing strongly in schools and youth tournaments."],
  'gen-engines': ["Using engines (Stockfish) to learn properly", "An engine tells you the best move but not why. A good way to learn: analyse by yourself first, write down your ideas, and only then turn on the engine to compare; at each difference, ask “what does the engine’s move threaten or prevent?” An evaluation of ±0.3 is close to equal, and small differences in the opening are nothing to worry about; focus on big swings in the score (blunders). On this site: Stockfish 19 grades every move in History, and mistakes go into the Mistake Notebook."],
  'tr-puzzles': ["Solving puzzles effectively", "Solving slowly and surely beats quick guessing: calculate the whole solution (including the opponent’s best replies) before the first move. The “woodpecker” method: solve the same puzzle set again and again, faster each round, so the patterns stick in your memory. A steady 10-20 minutes a day is better than two hours once a week."],
  'tr-visual': ["Training board visualisation", "Good visualisation helps you calculate further. Exercises: read a sequence of moves and imagine the position, then check on a board; name the colour of any square (for example e4 is a light square); solve mate-in-2 puzzles without moving the pieces. Start with positions with few pieces, then build up."],
  'tr-study-plan': ["A sample weekly training schedule", "Each day, 30-45 minutes: 15 minutes of tactics puzzles, 10 minutes of the Mistake Notebook or one Learn lesson, and the rest playing one game of 10 minutes or more. At the weekend: review 2-3 losses, learn a new endgame position, and revise an opening line you often meet. Note down repeated mistakes so you can focus on fixing them."],
  'tr-blunders': ["How to blunder less", "Most blunders come from: forgetting what the opponent’s last move threatens, forgetting which squares your pieces are guarding, and rushing when winning. Before each move, check quickly: after this move, does the opponent have a dangerous check, capture or threat? Which of my pieces are undefended? Sitting on your hands, writing the move down or counting to three before moving also helps."],
  'game-opera': ["The Opera Game (Morphy, 1858)", "Paul Morphy, playing White, faced the Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard (consulting together) during an opera in Paris in 1858. It is a model game of quick development and opening files against a king still in the middle: Morphy kept developing with tempo, sacrificed a knight and a rook, then the queen on move 16 to mate with a rook protected by a bishop (the “Opera mate” pattern). The lesson: do not waste moves trading a bishop for a knight early as Black did, and when the opponent is slow to develop, open files at once."],
  'game-immortal': ["The Immortal Game (Anderssen – Kieseritzky, 1851)", "Adolf Anderssen beat Lionel Kieseritzky in a friendly game in London in 1851, starting with the King’s Gambit. Anderssen sacrificed a bishop, two rooks and then the queen, and mated with his three remaining minor pieces (22.Qf6+! Nxf6 23.Be7#). It is the symbol of the 19th-century Romantic style: development and attack matter more than material. Today engines show that both sides made inaccurate moves, but the beauty of the finish is still remembered."],
  'game-evergreen': ["The Evergreen Game (Anderssen – Dufresne, 1852)", "Anderssen beat Jean Dufresne in Berlin in 1852 after an Evans Gambit. White sacrificed a pawn to open files, then unleashed a series of blows: 20.Rxe7+! and 21.Qxd7+!! sacrificing the queen, and the black king was driven by two bishops to mate on move 24. The game shows the power of open files and active bishops when the enemy king is stuck in the centre."],
  'game-legal': ["Légal’s Trap (Legall – Saint Brie, 1750)", "The most famous short game of the 18th century, played at the Café de la Régence in Paris. White “gifts” the queen with 5.Nxe5!, Black greedily takes 5...Bxd1?? and is mated two moves later by three minor pieces. The lesson: a relative pin does not hold a piece, and before taking big material, check your opponent’s checks."],
  'game-rubinstein': ["Rubinstein’s Immortal (Rotlewi – Rubinstein, 1907)", "Akiba Rubinstein, playing Black, beat Georg Rotlewi in Łódź in 1907 with one of the most beautiful sacrificial sequences in history: 22...Rxc3!, 23...Rd2!! and 24...Bxe4+, with two bishops aimed straight at the white king. The game is a lesson in the bishop pair on the long diagonals and the power of harmonious development before attacking."],
  'game-avro': ["Botvinnik – Capablanca (AVRO 1938)", "Mikhail Botvinnik beat former world champion José Raúl Capablanca at the AVRO tournament of 1938 (the Netherlands). From a Nimzo-Indian, White built a centre with e4-e5-f4-f5, then struck with 30.Ba3!! sacrificing a bishop to pull the black queen far away, and 31.Nh5+! clearing the way for the e-pawn to promote. It is a classic of central planning combined with a well-timed tactic."],
  'game-century': ["The Game of the Century (Byrne – Fischer, 1956)", "Bobby Fischer, then 13, playing Black, beat Donald Byrne in New York in 1956. The move 17...Be6!! gives up the queen in exchange for a string of White’s pieces through a windmill of discovered checks, and then Black’s minor pieces combine to drive the white king to mate. The game is a perfect example of judging a position by piece activity rather than by counting material."],
  'game-fischer-spassky6': ["Fischer – Spassky, Game 6 (Reykjavík 1972)", "Game 6 of the 1972 World Championship match. Fischer, who had played almost only 1.e4, surprised everyone with 1.c4 and played a perfect strategic game in the Tartakower variation of the Queen’s Gambit Declined: pushing e4-e5, locking the centre, then piling rooks on the f-file and breaking up Black’s kingside. It is said that Spassky himself stood and applauded after the game. Fischer won the match and became the first American world champion."],
  'game-short-timman': ["Short – Timman: the king walk (Tilburg 1991)", "Nigel Short beat Jan Timman at Tilburg 1991 with a rare idea: once White’s heavy pieces had locked down the black king, the white king itself walked Kh2-g3-f4-g5 to join the mating attack (Black resigned because Kh6 followed by Qg7# could not be stopped). The game shows that when the position is locked and the opponent has no checks, the king is an attacking piece too."],
  'game-deepblue': ["Deep Blue – Kasparov, Game 6 (New York 1997)", "The deciding game of the 1997 rematch: IBM’s Deep Blue supercomputer beat Garry Kasparov in 19 moves in a Caro-Kann Defence, thanks to the knight sacrifice 8.Nxe6 (Kasparov is thought to have mixed up the move order with ...h6). Deep Blue won the match 3.5 – 2.5, the first time a computer beat a world champion in a match at standard time controls."],
  'game-kasparov-topalov': ["Kasparov – Topalov: “Kasparov’s Immortal” (Wijk aan Zee 1999)", "Garry Kasparov beat Veselin Topalov at Wijk aan Zee in 1999 with the rook sacrifice 24.Rxd4!!, starting a king hunt across the whole board: the black king was driven from the queenside all the way to White’s first rank. Many consider it Kasparov’s most beautiful game, showing deep calculation and sacrificing material for a lasting initiative."],
  'gen-wch-history': ["The history of world championship matches", "The first official match in 1886: Steinitz beat Zukertort. 1927 in Buenos Aires: Alekhine beat Capablanca. 1948: FIDE held a tournament to choose a champion after Alekhine’s death, and Botvinnik took the title. 1972 in Reykjavík: Fischer beat Spassky during the Cold War. 1975: Fischer did not defend his title and Karpov became champion. 1984–85: the Karpov – Kasparov match was stopped after 48 games; in the 1985 rematch Kasparov won. 1993–2006: the title was split (PCA and FIDE), and reunified in 2006 when Kramnik beat Topalov. 2007: Anand became champion. 2013 in Chennai: Carlsen beat Anand and held the title until 2023. 2023: Carlsen did not play, and Ding Liren beat Nepomniachtchi. Late 2024 in Singapore: Gukesh Dommaraju (aged 18) beat Ding Liren and became the youngest world champion. (As of 2025.) Scores of recent matches (classical games, then rapid tie-breaks if any): 1972 Fischer beat Spassky 12.5–8.5. 1985 Kasparov beat Karpov 13–11 (Kasparov, aged 22, was the youngest champion until Gukesh). 2013 Carlsen beat Anand 6.5–3.5. 2014 Carlsen beat Anand 6.5–4.5. 2016 Carlsen – Karjakin drew 6–6, and Carlsen won the rapid tie-break 3–1. 2018 Carlsen – Caruana drew all 12 games, and Carlsen won the tie-break 3–0. 2021 Carlsen beat Nepomniachtchi 7.5–3.5. 2023 Ding Liren – Nepomniachtchi drew 7–7, and Ding won the rapid tie-break 2.5–1.5. 2024 Gukesh beat Ding Liren 7.5–6.5 with no tie-break needed: in game 14 (the last), Ding erred in a nearly drawn rook endgame and Gukesh won."],
  'gen-computers': ["Computers and chess", "In 1997, IBM’s Deep Blue supercomputer beat Garry Kasparov 3.5 – 2.5. Since then, programs such as Fritz, Rybka, Houdini, Komodo and Stockfish (open source) have left humans far behind. In 2017, DeepMind’s AlphaZero taught itself from the rules alone and beat Stockfish in a test match; the neural-network idea was carried on by the Leela Chess Zero project. Since 2020, Stockfish has used NNUE networks and is now among the strongest engines in the world; this site runs Stockfish 19 directly in the browser."],
  'gen-greats': ["Great players and their styles", "Morphy (19th century): master of development and attack. Capablanca: simple positions and near-perfect endgame technique. Alekhine: complex, imaginative attacks. Tal: the “Magician from Riga”, with daring sacrifices. Petrosian: prophylaxis, defence, exchange sacrifices. Fischer: precise and clear, with thorough opening preparation. Karpov: slow strategic strangulation. Kasparov: dynamic, deep opening preparation, powerful attacks. Carlsen: squeezing small advantages, superb endgames. Judit Polgár: the strongest woman player in history, once in the world top 10, with a sharp attacking style."],
  'gen-olympiad': ["The Olympiad and major tournaments", "The Chess Olympiad is a team event between nations, held every two years (the first official one in London in 1927). The Candidates Tournament selects the challenger to the world champion. Famous invitational events: Tata Steel (Wijk aan Zee, the Netherlands, every January), Norway Chess and the Sinquefield Cup. FIDE also runs the World Cup (knockout) and the World Rapid and Blitz Championships."],
};
